1
00:00:17,891 --> 00:00:21,773
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is Samyutta Nikaya 7.2.

2
00:00:23,794 --> 00:00:33,699
[SPEAKER_01]: And on one occasion, a Brahmin heard that another Brahmin had gone forth from the home life into homelessness in the presence of the Blessed One.

3
00:00:34,339 --> 00:00:37,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he sort of changed his religion there.

4
00:00:38,361 --> 00:00:45,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And this Brahmin, angered and displeased, went to the Blessed One and on arrival, insulted him with rude and harsh words.

5
00:00:49,032 --> 00:00:50,153
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he was telling the Buddha off.

6
00:00:50,493 --> 00:00:54,356
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the Buddha says, What do you think, Brahman?

7
00:00:55,317 --> 00:00:58,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Do friends or relatives come to you as guests?

8
00:00:59,761 --> 00:01:01,502
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yes, they occasionally do that.

9
00:01:02,283 --> 00:01:04,645
[SPEAKER_01]: And do you serve them with food and drinks?

10
00:01:04,925 --> 00:01:05,345
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, yes.

11
00:01:06,626 --> 00:01:10,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And if they don't accept those offerings, to whom do those offerings belong?

12
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:13,412
[SPEAKER_01]: If they don't accept them, the offerings still belong to me.

13
00:01:15,145 --> 00:01:29,109
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says, likewise, Brahman, we, who do not abuse anyone, who do not scold anyone, who do not quarrel against anyone, refuse to accept from you the abuse, the scolding and the quarrel that you let loose at us.

14
00:01:30,089 --> 00:01:33,330
[SPEAKER_01]: It still belongs to you.

15
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:43,613
[SPEAKER_01]: Brahman, one who abuses his abuser, who scolds the one who scolds him, who fights against the one who fights with him, he is said to partake of the meal,

16
00:01:44,581 --> 00:01:46,022
[SPEAKER_01]: to enter upon an exchange.

17
00:01:46,582 --> 00:01:48,944
[SPEAKER_01]: But we not, we do not partake of your meal.

18
00:01:49,564 --> 00:01:51,246
[SPEAKER_01]: We do not enter upon an exchange.

19
00:01:51,926 --> 00:01:52,967
[SPEAKER_01]: It still belongs to you.

20
00:01:54,288 --> 00:01:59,351
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Brahman says, says, well, like, ooh, you know.

21
00:02:00,972 --> 00:02:04,295
[SPEAKER_01]: The king, together with his consort, know this of the master Gautama.

22
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:07,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Gautama is the contemplative... Gautama is an arahant.

23
00:02:08,117 --> 00:02:10,099
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, master Gautama gets angry.

24
00:02:11,119 --> 00:02:11,680
[SPEAKER_03]: So he's like,

25
00:02:14,115 --> 00:02:17,957
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha replies, how can anger arise in one who's angerless?

26
00:02:18,757 --> 00:02:20,518
[SPEAKER_01]: In the tamed one of righteous living?

27
00:02:21,158 --> 00:02:23,079
[SPEAKER_01]: In one liberated by perfect knowledge?

28
00:02:24,039 --> 00:02:25,740
[SPEAKER_01]: In the stable one who abides in peace?

29
00:02:27,061 --> 00:02:31,803
[SPEAKER_01]: One who repays an angry man with anger, thereby makes things worse for himself.

30
00:02:32,783 --> 00:02:36,625
[SPEAKER_01]: Not repaying an angry man with anger, one wins a battle hard to win.

31
00:02:37,768 --> 00:02:42,711
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's a question, a few questions that I gathered from this, that others seem to be interested in.

32
00:03:09,068 --> 00:03:14,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Because whenever I meet a certain rude person, I wish they're metta.

33
00:03:15,050 --> 00:03:18,631
[SPEAKER_01]: But as much as I have done this, that person still remains rude.

34
00:03:19,952 --> 00:03:24,793
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is the power of metta not working?

35
00:03:24,813 --> 00:03:26,194
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the first question.

36
00:03:26,754 --> 00:03:27,574
[SPEAKER_05]: How would you answer that?

37
00:03:27,594 --> 00:03:30,535
[SPEAKER_04]: Because it's not metta, what that means.

38
00:03:30,615 --> 00:03:33,716
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, that's correct.

39
00:03:33,916 --> 00:03:35,317
[SPEAKER_05]: And any other way?

40
00:03:36,952 --> 00:03:45,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, if metta is supposed to make the other person not be rude anymore, then... Yeah, exactly.

41
00:03:45,636 --> 00:03:48,538
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, metta is not working, because that's not where metta is supposed to work.

42
00:03:50,619 --> 00:03:53,240
[SPEAKER_03]: It's as simple as that.

43
00:03:53,460 --> 00:04:03,325
[SPEAKER_05]: Friendliness is your framework of your mind, it's your attitude, it's your cultivation in face of rudeness and so on.

44
00:04:05,234 --> 00:04:10,718
[SPEAKER_05]: And the whole point of practicing metta is so that you don't give rise to anger or ill-will.

45
00:04:12,079 --> 00:04:18,865
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter how long the external abuse, rudeness and so on lasts.

46
00:04:19,105 --> 00:04:20,366
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not... that's not your problem.

47
00:04:21,667 --> 00:04:25,851
[SPEAKER_01]: It's... and it sounds to me like this person is using metta as a retaliation, you know.

48
00:04:26,551 --> 00:04:29,794
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, no, it's you using metta as a way out of the unpleasant experience.

49
00:04:30,354 --> 00:04:33,217
[SPEAKER_05]: I'll do metta, so they'll stop being angry, so we're all friends again.

50
00:04:34,337 --> 00:04:34,577
[SPEAKER_05]: Why?

51
00:04:34,717 --> 00:04:46,628
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, because I dislike the discomfort of experiencing rudeness, which means I'm moved by it, I'm pressured by it, I'm not at peace internally when I experience those things.

52
00:04:46,668 --> 00:04:48,710
[SPEAKER_05]: So, matter should shelter me from those things.

53
00:04:48,770 --> 00:04:49,010
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

54
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:52,573
[SPEAKER_05]: Matter should make you immovable in regard to those things.

55
00:04:52,714 --> 00:04:53,074
[SPEAKER_05]: That's it.

56
00:04:58,127 --> 00:05:15,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, what I meant is, like, someone is having thoughts of kindness or trying to spread metta energy to the person, but that that whole attitude is one of trying to, like, attack or retaliate against that rudeness, rather than... Yeah, it's acting out, sure.

57
00:05:15,963 --> 00:05:16,763
[SPEAKER_05]: It's acting out, yeah.

58
00:05:17,304 --> 00:05:19,165
[SPEAKER_05]: It's partaking in that meal, just in a different way.

59
00:05:19,185 --> 00:05:19,586
[SPEAKER_04]: Which is not metta.

60
00:05:19,706 --> 00:05:19,886
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

61
00:05:20,787 --> 00:05:25,091
[SPEAKER_05]: But you're still doing it because you're experiencing the unpleasantness of rudeness.

62
00:05:32,355 --> 00:05:45,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, another kind of question that somebody might ask is like, if I'm doing Metta properly, I shouldn't be... it shouldn't even be uncomfortable.

63
00:05:45,459 --> 00:05:49,121
[SPEAKER_00]: It shouldn't be unpleasant for somebody to insult me or something.

64
00:05:49,161 --> 00:05:51,622
[SPEAKER_00]: I should just not care at all.

65
00:05:52,722 --> 00:05:53,382
[SPEAKER_00]: Is that the case?

66
00:05:56,292 --> 00:06:04,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Or can it still be, like, because you said, you don't want the discomfort of the anger, that's why you act out.

67
00:06:05,014 --> 00:06:10,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But somebody might say, oh, but if I was doing it right, it shouldn't be uncomfortable at all.

68
00:06:11,595 --> 00:06:12,955
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not because I don't want...

69
00:06:13,215 --> 00:06:15,216
[SPEAKER_05]: Right, right, right, right, right.

70
00:06:15,236 --> 00:06:16,596
[SPEAKER_05]: Not at all.

71
00:06:16,616 --> 00:06:18,596
[SPEAKER_05]: The abusive words will remain abusive words.

72
00:06:19,297 --> 00:06:21,177
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it will be like heat, cold,

73
00:06:22,124 --> 00:06:24,206
[SPEAKER_05]: Rain, wind, abusive words.

74
00:06:24,706 --> 00:06:33,515
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's going to be uncomfortable, but none of that discomfort can move the mind, or can make the mind re-engage willingly with ill-will.

75
00:06:34,515 --> 00:06:37,478
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it will remain imperturbable.

76
00:06:37,498 --> 00:06:41,041
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just because the Buddha doesn't get angry in this case, doesn't mean it was like...

77
00:06:41,081 --> 00:06:46,126
[SPEAKER_05]: He's unaware of the actions of that Brahmin, that he was rude and offensive.

78
00:06:46,466 --> 00:06:47,067
[SPEAKER_03]: No, no, of course.

79
00:06:50,109 --> 00:06:56,353
[SPEAKER_05]: If somebody comes in this room and I'm showering them with attitude of friendliness, is that spreading of metta?

80
00:06:56,513 --> 00:06:56,693
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

81
00:06:57,373 --> 00:07:00,235
[SPEAKER_04]: What is that then?

82
00:07:00,635 --> 00:07:01,836
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the showering then?

83
00:07:02,016 --> 00:07:08,239
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, like I'm sort of welcoming, doing all these things just to make them feel my friendship.

84
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:12,182
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's acting out of craving for...

85
00:07:12,322 --> 00:07:13,762
[SPEAKER_04]: So...

86
00:07:14,063 --> 00:07:16,504
[SPEAKER_05]: So then, when the Buddha says he

87
00:07:19,705 --> 00:07:20,465
[SPEAKER_05]: Spreads matter.

88
00:07:21,026 --> 00:07:21,486
[SPEAKER_05]: Pervades.

89
00:07:21,746 --> 00:07:23,207
[SPEAKER_05]: Pervades the entire world with it.

90
00:07:24,327 --> 00:07:25,848
[SPEAKER_05]: Pervades all the quarters with it.

91
00:07:27,769 --> 00:07:28,509
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that not the same?

92
00:07:30,910 --> 00:07:31,911
[SPEAKER_05]: Pervading the world.

93
00:07:34,032 --> 00:07:35,853
[SPEAKER_05]: Pervading individuals.

94
00:07:35,873 --> 00:07:36,273
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it is.

95
00:07:36,293 --> 00:07:37,193
[SPEAKER_04]: Pervading the groups.

96
00:07:39,895 --> 00:07:40,795
[SPEAKER_04]: Like a negative.

97
00:07:41,896 --> 00:07:45,357
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's pervading and spreading, what I refer to.

98
00:07:45,377 --> 00:07:48,239
[SPEAKER_05]: Is that the same or different?

99
00:07:48,279 --> 00:07:48,639
[SPEAKER_04]: Different.

100
00:07:49,712 --> 00:07:51,093
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is pervading, then?

101
00:07:52,734 --> 00:07:54,416
[SPEAKER_05]: How is pervading different from spreading?

102
00:07:56,117 --> 00:07:59,740
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, um, colloquially and, um... No, practically.

103
00:08:00,120 --> 00:08:07,786
[SPEAKER_05]: So, say, we describe spreading of metta, showing your friendliness, engaging with it, thinking positive thoughts, all that.

104
00:08:08,787 --> 00:08:11,109
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what is pervading, then, if it's different than that?

105
00:08:12,330 --> 00:08:13,491
[SPEAKER_05]: How would you do pervading?

106
00:08:14,371 --> 00:08:19,175
[SPEAKER_05]: How would you pervade the world with your attitude of friendliness?

107
00:08:20,330 --> 00:08:23,412
[SPEAKER_04]: Where is that done?

108
00:08:23,512 --> 00:08:23,892
[SPEAKER_04]: Exactly.

109
00:08:24,092 --> 00:08:24,452
[SPEAKER_05]: Finished.

110
00:08:24,812 --> 00:08:25,293
[SPEAKER_02]: Internally.

111
00:08:25,693 --> 00:08:31,856
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not done in the world that you assume of others, pervading the quarters.

112
00:08:32,156 --> 00:08:37,039
[SPEAKER_05]: It's all done internally and it requires no acting out.

113
00:08:38,379 --> 00:08:41,741
[SPEAKER_05]: It requires no overly showing these attitudes.

114
00:08:43,125 --> 00:08:47,587
[SPEAKER_05]: Which are all rooted exactly in craving and wanting other person to be affected by your friendliness.

115
00:08:48,067 --> 00:08:48,887
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's not matter.

116
00:08:48,907 --> 00:08:50,327
[SPEAKER_05]: Matter is on the level of the mind.

117
00:08:52,968 --> 00:08:58,490
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the up, down, east, west, in every direction.

118
00:08:59,090 --> 00:09:00,851
[SPEAKER_05]: Do you have to travel physically to... No.

119
00:09:00,971 --> 00:09:02,992
[SPEAKER_05]: You already are doing it internally.

120
00:09:03,012 --> 00:09:04,092
[SPEAKER_05]: It just gets conflated.

121
00:09:04,192 --> 00:09:08,274
[SPEAKER_05]: That type of pervading gets conflated with all these external acts.

122
00:09:08,534 --> 00:09:10,014
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, see, this is what is meant by that.

123
00:09:10,054 --> 00:09:12,315
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's like I'm pervading, I'm visualizing,

124
00:09:12,882 --> 00:09:17,124
[SPEAKER_05]: And then I'm expressing it to everyone, everything, all the time.

125
00:09:17,824 --> 00:09:25,628
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's completely not what the matter is, because the same attitude of pervading, you know, you cultivate for all four brahma-vihāras.

126
00:09:25,648 --> 00:09:26,108
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

127
00:09:26,428 --> 00:09:28,849
[SPEAKER_05]: So, how are you going to express your indifference?

128
00:09:28,989 --> 00:09:29,269
[SPEAKER_05]: Equanimity.

129
00:09:29,409 --> 00:09:30,010
[SPEAKER_05]: Equanimity.

130
00:09:30,250 --> 00:09:32,851
[SPEAKER_05]: How are you going to pervade that onto the others?

131
00:09:32,931 --> 00:09:33,511
[SPEAKER_05]: Or spread that.

132
00:09:33,531 --> 00:09:34,091
[SPEAKER_05]: Spread them.

133
00:09:34,392 --> 00:09:35,372
[SPEAKER_05]: Spread equanimity.

134
00:09:36,420 --> 00:09:37,500
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, well, that's not gonna be...

135
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:38,201
[SPEAKER_05]: Spread immovability.

136
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:39,041
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not spreadable.

137
00:09:39,521 --> 00:09:39,741
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

138
00:09:40,102 --> 00:09:40,482
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

139
00:09:41,162 --> 00:09:42,943
[SPEAKER_05]: But it is... you can pervade it.

140
00:09:43,803 --> 00:09:43,983
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

141
00:09:44,063 --> 00:09:51,106
[SPEAKER_05]: You can pervade all quarters in your mind, in all directions, as far as your thought goes, with that attitude.

142
00:09:52,827 --> 00:09:59,750
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's a completely an internal practice, which is why exactly it will establish you in the dwelling of Brahmas.

143
00:10:00,370 --> 00:10:01,530
[SPEAKER_05]: No matter what anyone does.

144
00:10:01,550 --> 00:10:01,851
[SPEAKER_05]: No matter.

145
00:10:02,031 --> 00:10:04,772
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if you don't do anything externally, you just sit silently.

146
00:10:06,784 --> 00:10:20,696
[SPEAKER_05]: Of course, if you spread such attitude internally, not externally, if you pervade all the quarters with that attitude, then, doesn't matter what comes through your senses, or who comes through your senses, that attitude will not be able to be perturbed.

147
00:10:21,196 --> 00:10:23,358
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's how you've successfully cultivated metta.

148
00:10:24,279 --> 00:10:27,782
[SPEAKER_05]: When you're not perturbed by things, they would want to perturb you with ill-will and so on.

149
00:10:30,592 --> 00:10:33,758
[SPEAKER_05]: And it has absolutely nothing to do with other person, persons, at all.

150
00:10:34,339 --> 00:10:37,505
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, it's conditional on their actions, so... Yeah.

151
00:10:38,106 --> 00:10:40,971
[SPEAKER_05]: We said that before in those other talks, like, you know, so...

152
00:10:42,360 --> 00:10:48,504
[SPEAKER_05]: When that, eh... when the Buddha says, and then you protect that state of mind, as a mother would protect a child.

153
00:10:49,404 --> 00:10:50,985
[SPEAKER_05]: No, not protect random people.

154
00:10:51,566 --> 00:10:52,366
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not the problem.

155
00:10:52,826 --> 00:10:54,227
[SPEAKER_05]: Mind of ill-will is your problem.

156
00:10:54,267 --> 00:10:59,891
[SPEAKER_05]: Being perturbed away from the wholesome state of mind of friendliness is your problem.

157
00:11:00,271 --> 00:11:01,231
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's where you stay.

158
00:11:02,332 --> 00:11:05,114
[SPEAKER_05]: Guarding that.

159
00:11:05,174 --> 00:11:09,997
[SPEAKER_01]: So, another question is that I hear many people voicing their wrong views.

160
00:11:10,798 --> 00:11:12,199
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you partake in other people's actions?

161
00:11:39,658 --> 00:11:47,283
[SPEAKER_05]: In other people's internal choices, are beings owners of their own actions, or they own a bit of their own actions, a bit of someone else's actions?

162
00:11:47,303 --> 00:11:47,383
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes.

163
00:11:48,304 --> 00:11:49,645
[SPEAKER_05]: So, it's not a problem.

164
00:11:50,385 --> 00:11:51,686
[SPEAKER_05]: None of that can be a problem.

165
00:11:51,706 --> 00:11:57,370
[SPEAKER_05]: You are making it a problem by not being able to tolerate it, by not being able to not act out of it.

166
00:12:00,392 --> 00:12:04,975
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you act out of your own craving, because other people hold all these wrong views rooted in craving.

167
00:12:06,576 --> 00:12:08,057
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you got your answer there.

168
00:12:08,264 --> 00:12:09,625
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, you could nearly just...

169
00:12:10,286 --> 00:12:18,933
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you happen to hear other people or see other people doing some things that you find misguided or that you disagree with.

170
00:12:20,254 --> 00:12:20,474
[SPEAKER_00]: But...

171
00:12:21,475 --> 00:12:25,699
[SPEAKER_05]: Assuming your duty to be... To be to correct those things.

172
00:12:25,739 --> 00:12:29,922
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if you kind of can take that to its conclusion, well, you're just going to have to...

173
00:12:30,182 --> 00:12:30,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Endless.

174
00:12:30,763 --> 00:12:31,403
[SPEAKER_00]: It's endless.

175
00:12:31,423 --> 00:12:36,868
[SPEAKER_05]: Like you spend your entire life circling... Because vast majority will always remain in darkness, will always remain...

176
00:12:37,373 --> 00:12:45,520
[SPEAKER_05]: With wrong views, we'll always remain misrepresenting the things, so... Enlightenment is not about correcting everybody else, just about fixing your own issue.

177
00:12:46,201 --> 00:12:47,522
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's how you practice for both.

178
00:12:49,644 --> 00:12:55,529
[SPEAKER_05]: But that's a hard thing to accept, for somebody who still doesn't want to relinquish their own righteous craving.

179
00:12:58,903 --> 00:13:01,785
[SPEAKER_05]: I mean, you say, Arahant practices for both, by becoming enlightened himself.

180
00:13:01,865 --> 00:13:04,347
[SPEAKER_05]: No, no, but no, you must be more engaged with the world.

181
00:13:04,788 --> 00:13:08,571
[SPEAKER_05]: You must bring social change, you must affect, you must correct them.

182
00:13:09,511 --> 00:13:10,272
[SPEAKER_05]: Actually, no, you don't.

183
00:13:10,372 --> 00:13:13,655
[SPEAKER_05]: You can, if you want to, but you better be aware of the reasons why you're doing it.

184
00:13:14,175 --> 00:13:19,399
[SPEAKER_05]: Because they might well be the same reasons that these people have, because of which they're in such darkness.

185
00:13:19,840 --> 00:13:21,061
[SPEAKER_05]: Craving and desire.

186
00:13:23,042 --> 00:13:24,263
[SPEAKER_05]: Righteousness and blindness.

187
00:13:25,184 --> 00:13:25,404
[SPEAKER_05]: So...

188
00:13:27,385 --> 00:13:48,068
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that sutta, I think it's in the Majjhima Nikaya, where the Buddha is talking to two householders, lay people, and one of them, after a certain amount of time, says, okay, I have to go now, and he leaves, and then the Buddha says, you know, if he had stayed a bit longer, he would have seen the Dhamma and attained the right view.

189
00:13:49,474 --> 00:13:50,474
[SPEAKER_00]: But he didn't say anything.

190
00:13:50,494 --> 00:13:51,335
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, it wasn't his.

191
00:13:51,455 --> 00:13:52,055
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

192
00:13:52,515 --> 00:13:53,356
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't even say it.

193
00:13:53,836 --> 00:13:56,457
[SPEAKER_00]: And when the guy was right in front of him, maybe you should stay a bit longer.

194
00:13:56,497 --> 00:13:58,618
[SPEAKER_00]: It was up to him to go.

195
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is a kind of similar question.

196
00:14:06,241 --> 00:14:16,305
[SPEAKER_01]: I sometimes get annoyed by how others speak to me and feel that they should at least learn how to address others correctly, so that the world in general is a better place.

197
00:14:17,203 --> 00:14:27,906
[SPEAKER_01]: I try to wish them well, but deep down I'm still annoyed, and despite my well wishes, when I engage with the person, my annoyance is what shows itself, and not my good intentions.

198
00:14:27,946 --> 00:14:30,047
[SPEAKER_05]: Because that's the main reason for your action.

199
00:14:30,727 --> 00:14:34,649
[SPEAKER_01]: So what should I do to better prepare myself for the next time someone says something annoying?

200
00:14:35,749 --> 00:14:39,050
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, give up the idea of the world becoming a better place, to begin with.

201
00:14:40,386 --> 00:14:42,868
[SPEAKER_05]: Because why is the world not a better place?

202
00:14:42,908 --> 00:14:46,171
[SPEAKER_05]: Is it because people speak bad things?

203
00:14:46,691 --> 00:14:47,292
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, okay.

204
00:14:48,033 --> 00:14:49,594
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do people speak bad things?

205
00:14:50,214 --> 00:14:51,556
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do people do bad things?

206
00:14:52,176 --> 00:14:53,057
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do people kill?

207
00:14:53,918 --> 00:14:54,818
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do people lie?

208
00:14:55,439 --> 00:15:04,367
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do people steal, cheat, seek gain at the expense of others, enslave others, and all the other misfortune in the world?

209
00:15:04,447 --> 00:15:05,368
[SPEAKER_05]: Why do they do that?

210
00:15:07,129 --> 00:15:07,830
[SPEAKER_05]: It's strange.

211
00:15:08,891 --> 00:15:12,233
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, because of desire, because of central desire, that's it.

212
00:15:13,434 --> 00:15:14,515
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you want to fix the world?

213
00:15:14,815 --> 00:15:15,616
[SPEAKER_05]: That's where you fix it.

214
00:15:16,076 --> 00:15:21,360
[SPEAKER_05]: You don't fix it by just making everybody comply to your worldview, even if it's a right worldview.

215
00:15:22,521 --> 00:15:30,026
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not going to work, because things you're making them comply with, were never the problem, they were the result of the problem of desire.

216
00:15:30,146 --> 00:15:31,667
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's what we said, when

217
00:15:32,697 --> 00:15:38,719
[SPEAKER_05]: Sons fight with fathers, mothers with fathers, husbands and daughters and kings with other kings and everybody.

218
00:15:38,799 --> 00:15:42,960
[SPEAKER_05]: The whole world fights because of central desire, because of desires.

219
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:44,240
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

220
00:15:44,260 --> 00:15:45,701
[SPEAKER_01]: The hate for pain.

221
00:15:46,101 --> 00:15:47,181
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, that all comes with it.

222
00:15:47,261 --> 00:15:52,202
[SPEAKER_05]: But basically, for as long as you're acting out of your desires, the world will be the way the world is.

223
00:15:52,563 --> 00:15:54,443
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you want to make a world a better place?

224
00:15:55,303 --> 00:15:57,284
[SPEAKER_05]: Start uprooting the desire.

225
00:15:58,164 --> 00:15:59,224
[SPEAKER_05]: Where do you start at?

226
00:15:59,284 --> 00:16:00,345
[SPEAKER_05]: In others or in yourself?

227
00:16:00,665 --> 00:16:00,885
[SPEAKER_05]: Well,

228
00:16:02,067 --> 00:16:03,848
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, you don't really need to answer that question.

229
00:16:04,468 --> 00:16:10,071
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you will be factually making a world a better place if you free yourself from sensual desire.

230
00:16:10,111 --> 00:16:18,895
[SPEAKER_05]: Means there is one being less in this world, massive world, fueled with desire, where there is one person less that has that desire.

231
00:16:19,916 --> 00:16:21,436
[SPEAKER_05]: One desire has been extinguished.

232
00:16:22,297 --> 00:16:23,938
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that is factually an improvement.

233
00:16:25,238 --> 00:16:26,359
[SPEAKER_05]: So, one step at a time.

234
00:16:27,099 --> 00:16:28,600
[SPEAKER_05]: And then teach others how to do the same.

235
00:16:31,272 --> 00:16:33,474
[SPEAKER_05]: But then, by then, you would have recognized where the problem is.

236
00:16:33,854 --> 00:16:35,335
[SPEAKER_05]: So, the problem is not the state of the world.

237
00:16:35,735 --> 00:16:38,477
[SPEAKER_05]: That's just the result of the problem, which is the desire.

238
00:16:39,097 --> 00:16:48,604
[SPEAKER_05]: So, for as long as you're acting out of the same desires, even righteously, you're still adding the same fuel to the same world, to the same problem of it.

239
00:16:48,644 --> 00:16:57,170
[SPEAKER_01]: That view said that the world will be a... get to a stage where it's just a wonderful place, a peaceful place, everyone's happy.

240
00:16:57,230 --> 00:16:58,251
[SPEAKER_05]: That's just your desire, again.

241
00:17:00,056 --> 00:17:00,896
[SPEAKER_05]: And somebody else will come.

242
00:17:00,956 --> 00:17:01,437
[SPEAKER_05]: I don't want to.

243
00:17:01,877 --> 00:17:02,757
[SPEAKER_05]: I like the world the way it is.

244
00:17:03,358 --> 00:17:04,038
[SPEAKER_05]: That's my design.

245
00:17:04,798 --> 00:17:05,399
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what do we do now?

246
00:17:05,479 --> 00:17:06,059
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, we fight.

247
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:10,861
[SPEAKER_05]: Sooner or later, it will have to lead to that.

248
00:17:11,622 --> 00:17:13,783
[SPEAKER_02]: And then, the other one wins, if you end up fighting him.

249
00:17:14,883 --> 00:17:16,404
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, whoever wins means...

250
00:17:16,464 --> 00:17:24,948
[SPEAKER_05]: Anyway, you made the world a better place by fighting and violence, which is always what people resort to, eventually, even the most well-wishing.

251
00:17:26,485 --> 00:17:29,447
[SPEAKER_05]: Making the world a better place, well, nothing seems to work.

252
00:17:29,527 --> 00:17:31,789
[SPEAKER_05]: Let's just resort to force.

253
00:17:34,471 --> 00:17:38,234
[SPEAKER_00]: It's actually quite useful.

254
00:17:40,884 --> 00:17:57,122
[SPEAKER_00]: Contemplation, in a way, because, you know, you can see the things that people do to each other on a very grand scale, like all kinds of corruption, thievery, whatever.

255
00:17:57,382 --> 00:18:00,906
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can look at it and be disgusted by it.

256
00:18:01,026 --> 00:18:01,867
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very obvious.

257
00:18:04,526 --> 00:18:10,073
[SPEAKER_00]: But, when you, if you see, if you realize, oh, like, what's the cause of this?

258
00:18:10,374 --> 00:18:21,208
[SPEAKER_00]: This person is, you know, their actions happen to have these grand repercussions on a huge scale that affect thousands of people because they're in a position of power or something, or it's exactly the same thing.

259
00:18:21,789 --> 00:18:22,209
[SPEAKER_00]: Thus...

260
00:18:22,970 --> 00:18:30,935
[SPEAKER_00]: That drives me, that makes me act towards something I want, or try to get away, or try to deal with something I dislike.

261
00:18:31,655 --> 00:18:39,580
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, even that disgust with the world can kind of make you, oh, like, I should restrain this, because it's the same thing.

262
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:45,383
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that person probably felt the same disgust, which is why he decided to conquer that whole culture and destroy it, and so on.

263
00:18:46,384 --> 00:18:51,527
[SPEAKER_05]: So, just acting out of the same reasons, completely covering it up.

264
00:18:52,465 --> 00:18:53,666
[SPEAKER_03]: Covering up the root of the problem.

265
00:18:57,548 --> 00:19:01,590
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're doing the same thing, you're, yeah, as that sutta says, you're partaking in exchange.

266
00:19:01,610 --> 00:19:02,991
[SPEAKER_01]: You're both doing the same thing.

267
00:19:03,091 --> 00:19:03,811
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, he started it.

268
00:19:03,831 --> 00:19:04,491
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter.

269
00:19:05,312 --> 00:19:07,953
[SPEAKER_05]: From your point of view, you are the one starting it for yourself.

270
00:19:08,514 --> 00:19:11,355
[SPEAKER_05]: He can't start it for you.

271
00:19:11,375 --> 00:19:16,898
[SPEAKER_05]: No amount of abuse can start abuse, can start, can make you an abuser.

272
00:19:17,078 --> 00:19:20,360
[SPEAKER_05]: Only your actions can then take you in that direction.

273
00:19:22,427 --> 00:19:24,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody can make you get angry.

274
00:19:24,228 --> 00:19:24,588
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

275
00:19:26,949 --> 00:19:29,851
[SPEAKER_05]: And certainly nobody can make you act out of that anger further.

276
00:19:32,152 --> 00:19:38,996
[SPEAKER_02]: There's this verse in the Dhammapada, you know, as long as somebody thinks, oh, he struck me, he abused me, he reviled me.

277
00:19:39,496 --> 00:19:43,458
[SPEAKER_02]: If you do not give up those thoughts, you will not be able to abandon ill will.

278
00:19:44,079 --> 00:19:44,279
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

279
00:19:44,539 --> 00:19:45,339
[SPEAKER_02]: Something along those lines.

280
00:19:45,660 --> 00:19:45,800
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

281
00:19:47,220 --> 00:19:49,001
[SPEAKER_05]: And the quarrels in the world will never end.

282
00:19:49,842 --> 00:19:50,162
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

283
00:19:50,988 --> 00:19:59,355
[SPEAKER_01]: This is at the Sutta of the Acrobats, Samyutta Nikaya 47.19.

284
00:20:01,436 --> 00:20:13,646
[SPEAKER_01]: The Blessed One said, once a bamboo acrobat, having erected a bamboo pole, addressed his assistant, come, climb up the bamboo pole and stand on my shoulders.

285
00:20:14,707 --> 00:20:20,111
[SPEAKER_01]: As you say, Master, the assistant answered, and climbing the bamboo pole stood on his shoulders.

286
00:20:21,295 --> 00:20:26,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the bamboo acrobat said to his assistant, now you watch after me and I'll watch after you.

287
00:20:27,704 --> 00:20:36,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, protecting each other, watching after each other, we'll show off our skill, receive our reward and come down safely from the bamboo pole.

288
00:20:37,613 --> 00:20:42,856
[SPEAKER_01]: When he had said this, the assistant said to him, but that won't do at all, Master.

289
00:20:43,356 --> 00:20:45,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You watch after yourself, and I'll watch after myself.

290
00:20:46,017 --> 00:20:54,362
[SPEAKER_01]: And thus, with each of us protecting ourselves, watching after ourselves, we'll show off our skill, receive our reward, and come down safely.

291
00:20:56,823 --> 00:21:02,526
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said, what the assistant said to her master was the right way in that case.

292
00:21:05,882 --> 00:21:11,425
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, for the safeguarding of oneself, satipaṭṭhāna should be practiced.

293
00:21:11,485 --> 00:21:15,528
[SPEAKER_01]: For the safeguarding of others, satipaṭṭhāna should be practiced.

294
00:21:15,568 --> 00:21:17,989
[SPEAKER_01]: When safeguarding yourself, you safeguard others.

295
00:21:19,390 --> 00:21:21,431
[SPEAKER_01]: When safeguarding others, you safeguard yourself.

296
00:21:22,612 --> 00:21:22,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

297
00:21:23,292 --> 00:21:26,314
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and how, yeah, it does say, explains it.

298
00:21:26,554 --> 00:21:28,015
[SPEAKER_05]: But no, I won't talk about it.

299
00:21:28,355 --> 00:21:29,276
[SPEAKER_05]: I want just an answer.

300
00:21:29,436 --> 00:21:31,397
[SPEAKER_01]: When safeguarding yourself, you safeguard others.

301
00:21:32,105 --> 00:21:38,267
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, so why, by safeguarding yourself with mindfulness, you are automatically safeguarding others?

302
00:21:38,947 --> 00:21:40,308
[SPEAKER_05]: How are others not safe?

303
00:21:42,768 --> 00:21:44,069
[SPEAKER_03]: By my acting out of it?

304
00:21:44,689 --> 00:21:48,950
[SPEAKER_05]: By anyone's acting out of your will, greed, aversion and delusion, factually.

305
00:21:49,291 --> 00:21:59,594
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there is always... there cannot be no... there cannot be any other route of that non-safety of others, harm towards others.

306
00:22:00,627 --> 00:22:14,895
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if everybody would actually just guard themselves, there would be no quarreling, there would be no violence, there would be no abuse, and others would be automatically... the result of that would be safety of others.

307
00:22:17,056 --> 00:22:28,803
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if the root of every abuse, every violence, every kind of suffering inflicted upon others is guarded by everybody individually,

308
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:30,900
[SPEAKER_03]: That's it.

309
00:22:31,580 --> 00:22:33,481
[SPEAKER_05]: The world factually becomes a better place.

310
00:22:33,901 --> 00:22:43,204
[SPEAKER_05]: Because there are no more expressions of anger, ill-will, hatred, and so on, and desires, in that sense as well.

311
00:22:43,224 --> 00:22:44,644
[SPEAKER_01]: The world is responsible.

312
00:22:45,444 --> 00:22:46,745
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, but it's just factually.

313
00:22:46,765 --> 00:22:49,466
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not even kind of, oh yeah, we guide ourselves in the world.

314
00:22:49,486 --> 00:22:49,586
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

315
00:22:49,646 --> 00:22:54,327
[SPEAKER_05]: Factually, when others are not safe, it's because somebody is acting against that.

316
00:22:55,287 --> 00:22:59,809
[SPEAKER_05]: And those acts can only be rooted in greed, aversion or distraction.

317
00:23:00,678 --> 00:23:06,323
[SPEAKER_05]: If you guard yourself against those, you cannot possibly come near those acts, they would endanger others.

318
00:23:07,303 --> 00:23:10,226
[SPEAKER_05]: So, factually, the world would be a safer place.

319
00:23:11,527 --> 00:23:14,309
[SPEAKER_02]: So, where does this practically start, for the average?

320
00:23:15,070 --> 00:23:16,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Not killing, for example.

321
00:23:16,211 --> 00:23:17,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Precepts, yeah.

322
00:23:17,331 --> 00:23:17,692
[SPEAKER_05]: Exactly.

323
00:23:17,812 --> 00:23:21,155
[SPEAKER_05]: Not killing, not stealing, not cheating, not harming, not lying.

324
00:23:21,415 --> 00:23:25,198
[SPEAKER_02]: It actually says that, like by observing these pañca-sīla.

325
00:23:25,678 --> 00:23:25,878
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

326
00:23:25,998 --> 00:23:27,840
[SPEAKER_02]: One gives immeasurable safety to the world.

327
00:23:28,100 --> 00:23:29,081
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

328
00:23:29,666 --> 00:23:32,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Others can trust that you won't kill them, or...

329
00:23:32,988 --> 00:23:40,872
[SPEAKER_05]: Even if they don't trust, factually, to the extent that you're guarding yourself, to that extent, there is no expression of unwholesome towards others.

330
00:23:42,132 --> 00:23:44,634
[SPEAKER_05]: So, factually, the world is a better place to that degree.

331
00:23:51,458 --> 00:24:01,409
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're looking after your bodily behavior, your verbal behavior, a person can still be quite... can still be give rise to that unwholesome intentions.

332
00:24:02,290 --> 00:24:02,470
[SPEAKER_05]: Who?

333
00:24:02,490 --> 00:24:03,091
[SPEAKER_05]: That person?

334
00:24:03,151 --> 00:24:03,351
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

335
00:24:03,712 --> 00:24:04,933
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah.

336
00:24:05,494 --> 00:24:10,419
[SPEAKER_02]: Unwholesome intentions, but unwholesome intention is not a danger to others.

337
00:24:11,520 --> 00:24:13,142
[SPEAKER_05]: Acting out of unwholesome intentions is.

338
00:24:14,435 --> 00:24:23,662
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if your sīla is unbroken, if that's where your effort is, if you restrain your senses, even if you have the most cruel intentions in your mind, you're not acting out of them.

339
00:24:24,403 --> 00:24:28,586
[SPEAKER_01]: But if now I'm cultivating those cruel intentions, I don't harm anyone.

340
00:24:29,507 --> 00:24:37,233
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, then you won't have internal peace, but you are still factually not contributing towards the suffering of the world, because you're not acting out of it.

341
00:24:39,059 --> 00:24:41,180
[SPEAKER_05]: But yes, eventually... Well, it depends.

342
00:24:41,460 --> 00:24:44,001
[SPEAKER_05]: Like, we're not talking about necessarily practice for arahantship.

343
00:24:44,021 --> 00:24:45,482
[SPEAKER_05]: We're talking about making the world a better place.

344
00:24:46,822 --> 00:24:51,844
[SPEAKER_05]: But yeah, if you want to be completely at peace, unable to even have such intentions, then yeah.

345
00:24:51,864 --> 00:24:54,245
[SPEAKER_05]: But even for that, you still need to restrain yourself first.

346
00:24:55,266 --> 00:25:00,088
[SPEAKER_05]: So, whether you have such intentions or not, the work that needs to be done beforehand remains unchanged.

347
00:25:02,418 --> 00:25:10,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if your intentions are clean, your mind is restrained, in that sense, then there is absolutely no possibility of acting out.

348
00:25:11,262 --> 00:25:17,225
[SPEAKER_05]: Yes, but in order for your intentions to be clean and mind restrained, you need to have thoroughly not acted out for a long time.

349
00:25:18,385 --> 00:25:18,985
[SPEAKER_05]: And that will.

350
00:25:19,486 --> 00:25:21,606
[SPEAKER_05]: And also, it won't be possible, to be honest.

351
00:25:21,907 --> 00:25:22,787
[SPEAKER_05]: It won't be possible.

352
00:25:22,827 --> 00:25:27,109
[SPEAKER_05]: Theoretically, you could see how you could still have thoughts of ill will and cruelty arising.

353
00:25:27,589 --> 00:25:31,931
[SPEAKER_05]: If you have been thoroughly restrained and withdrawn from unwholesome for a

354
00:25:32,304 --> 00:25:35,627
[SPEAKER_05]: You know, long period of time, mind will have to come down.

355
00:25:36,507 --> 00:25:44,614
[SPEAKER_05]: So those thoughts were besetting you, were coming up, because there was still an opportunity, a hope, that you might give rise to expressing them.

356
00:25:45,755 --> 00:25:48,677
[SPEAKER_05]: But once you haven't been giving rise, those thoughts will come down.

357
00:25:49,959 --> 00:25:52,561
[SPEAKER_05]: So, yeah, still the same work, same practice.

358
00:26:01,524 --> 00:26:03,324
[SPEAKER_01]: And a question in regard to this sutta.

359
00:26:04,365 --> 00:26:08,426
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, this attitude of the master acrobat seems like the correct one.

360
00:26:10,106 --> 00:26:11,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, he wouldn't be the master.

361
00:26:13,067 --> 00:26:16,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, I'll watch you, you watch me.

362
00:26:17,768 --> 00:26:20,889
[SPEAKER_01]: So, selfishness does result in harm to others.

363
00:26:22,049 --> 00:26:26,790
[SPEAKER_01]: And if we can just be unselfish and help others, putting ourselves second,

364
00:26:27,465 --> 00:26:31,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Then the society would be a better place, and we would get rid of conceit, which is basically anatta.

365
00:26:32,769 --> 00:26:34,910
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this not also a valid path to nibbāna?

366
00:26:35,150 --> 00:26:35,710
[SPEAKER_05]: Hmm, phew!

367
00:26:36,151 --> 00:26:37,332
[SPEAKER_05]: A number of contradictions there.

368
00:26:37,672 --> 00:26:39,433
[SPEAKER_05]: First of all, selfishness is not the reason.

369
00:26:39,713 --> 00:26:41,534
[SPEAKER_05]: Selfishness is the result, also.

370
00:26:42,395 --> 00:26:46,397
[SPEAKER_05]: You are selfish because of the desires, because of craving.

371
00:26:49,399 --> 00:26:54,123
[SPEAKER_05]: So, your selfishness is not the root of all the problems, so you just need to put yourself second.

372
00:26:54,243 --> 00:26:54,403
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

373
00:26:54,938 --> 00:26:58,339
[SPEAKER_05]: Whether you're first or second, you're selfish, because you're doing it, because you have a desire to do so.

374
00:26:59,020 --> 00:27:00,240
[SPEAKER_05]: So, I have a desire to be second.

375
00:27:00,820 --> 00:27:02,561
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, she has a desire to be second.

376
00:27:03,321 --> 00:27:04,182
[SPEAKER_05]: So, now we're going to quarrel.

377
00:27:06,623 --> 00:27:09,464
[SPEAKER_01]: We have the same desire, but... Yeah, who's the most humble?

378
00:27:09,504 --> 00:27:10,264
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

379
00:27:10,384 --> 00:27:12,565
[SPEAKER_05]: One of them can only be... one of us can be second.

380
00:27:12,585 --> 00:27:13,846
[SPEAKER_05]: We can both be the same.

381
00:27:14,826 --> 00:27:15,586
[SPEAKER_05]: So, that's the point.

382
00:27:16,287 --> 00:27:17,587
[SPEAKER_05]: That's not the root of the problem.

383
00:27:19,408 --> 00:27:20,728
[SPEAKER_05]: Selfishness is the root of the problem.

384
00:27:20,788 --> 00:27:22,549
[SPEAKER_05]: Put myself second, the world is fixed.

385
00:27:22,589 --> 00:27:24,230
[SPEAKER_05]: See, we all just put ourselves second,

386
00:27:24,942 --> 00:27:25,462
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, then what?

387
00:27:25,482 --> 00:27:26,462
[SPEAKER_05]: Then we are all first again.

388
00:27:28,703 --> 00:27:30,724
[SPEAKER_05]: We are all acting out of our desires to be second.

389
00:27:31,304 --> 00:27:31,624
[SPEAKER_05]: But no.

390
00:27:31,684 --> 00:27:34,645
[SPEAKER_05]: Desire is the root of the problem.

391
00:27:34,665 --> 00:27:35,825
[SPEAKER_05]: Craving is the root of the problem.

392
00:27:37,026 --> 00:27:38,426
[SPEAKER_05]: So, don't act out of that and then, yeah...

393
00:27:41,767 --> 00:27:44,548
[SPEAKER_05]: But I still... As I said, things will then become clear.

394
00:27:44,568 --> 00:27:49,310
[SPEAKER_05]: If you start practicing like this, you realize, yeah, this is how factually the world is... becomes a better place.

395
00:27:49,510 --> 00:27:53,931
[SPEAKER_05]: But the world will never become a better place on the level of the world.

396
00:27:54,675 --> 00:28:01,280
[SPEAKER_05]: Because people are not the same, they're not born with the same faculties, not born with the same intent, like states of mind, so to speak.

397
00:28:02,201 --> 00:28:07,726
[SPEAKER_05]: And some people just will never ever be able to understand these things, or accept them, or restrain themselves.

398
00:28:08,666 --> 00:28:12,650
[SPEAKER_05]: So, factually, majority of the world will always remain in darkness.

399
00:28:13,270 --> 00:28:14,271
[SPEAKER_05]: That's just the nature of it.

400
00:28:14,631 --> 00:28:15,792
[SPEAKER_05]: Much like the animal realm.

401
00:28:19,535 --> 00:28:22,097
[SPEAKER_05]: But to that extent, you recognize, even if so,

402
00:28:23,378 --> 00:28:31,781
[SPEAKER_05]: If I remove desire in this creature, and maybe if somebody asks me, I can teach them.

403
00:28:31,941 --> 00:28:33,501
[SPEAKER_05]: If not, well, that's still not my problem.

404
00:28:33,841 --> 00:28:39,603
[SPEAKER_05]: But just to the extent of fixing myself, fixing desire and craving here, the world is brighter to that extent.

405
00:28:40,884 --> 00:28:45,005
[SPEAKER_05]: And that is the most direct, the most verifiable influencing of the world.

406
00:28:46,686 --> 00:28:50,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says satipaṭṭhāna should be practiced to guard yourself.

407
00:28:53,400 --> 00:28:55,181
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're seeing things in the correct order?

408
00:28:55,421 --> 00:28:58,062
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, well, yeah, the right view, everything else that comes with it.

409
00:28:58,783 --> 00:29:04,666
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, in a way, I thought, there would be seeing yourself, yourself as second, in the right way.

410
00:29:04,886 --> 00:29:06,266
[SPEAKER_05]: Internally, sure, sure.

411
00:29:06,467 --> 00:29:09,708
[SPEAKER_05]: But it's not a question of choice, of I choose to put myself second.

412
00:29:09,728 --> 00:29:11,229
[SPEAKER_02]: You're just acting out of your desire.

413
00:29:13,290 --> 00:29:17,012
[SPEAKER_05]: But yeah, you can learn how to see yourself second, and then you won't be able to take offense with anyone.

414
00:29:17,632 --> 00:29:19,753
[SPEAKER_05]: Because you always see yourself as this creature over there.

415
00:29:20,233 --> 00:29:21,394
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not your problem, not yours.

416
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:43,773
[SPEAKER_00]: There's just, I think, that question also sort of, obviously, it kind of shows the different ways in which this, like, protecting oneself is meant, as in, you know, when in a world of

417
00:29:44,771 --> 00:29:45,672
[SPEAKER_00]: Greed and craving.

418
00:29:46,232 --> 00:29:58,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Protecting oneself naturally is assumed to mean, like, okay, okay, we're all at a table together and I'm just gonna, like, grab all of the best food for myself first and that's me protecting myself.

419
00:29:58,262 --> 00:30:00,904
[SPEAKER_00]: They say, oh, you shouldn't do that.

420
00:30:00,924 --> 00:30:05,628
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yeah, no, but that's obviously not what is being meant here by protecting oneself.

421
00:30:06,429 --> 00:30:07,370
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you know,

422
00:30:09,321 --> 00:30:11,162
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, so we should all share with one another.

423
00:30:11,202 --> 00:30:12,183
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, yeah, that's better.

424
00:30:12,583 --> 00:30:19,347
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in a sense, what this is meaning is that, like, you should protect yourself by seeing, oh, no, okay, is it...

425
00:30:19,367 --> 00:30:21,048
[SPEAKER_00]: I should just not act out of desire.

426
00:30:21,708 --> 00:30:24,870
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, for my own benefit, basically.

427
00:30:24,910 --> 00:30:28,032
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, as a secondary effect, means that I don't really...

428
00:30:28,132 --> 00:30:32,434
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not gonna try and grab everything to... Yeah.

429
00:30:32,954 --> 00:30:35,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might then even still do things like that somebody

430
00:30:37,320 --> 00:30:40,902
[SPEAKER_00]: With a different type of view, you might do like, for example, being generous, let's say.

431
00:30:41,563 --> 00:30:47,507
[SPEAKER_00]: You could do that, but you can do that as a kind of a, let me be generous and show how much I'm giving and helping.

432
00:30:47,527 --> 00:30:47,827
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

433
00:30:48,027 --> 00:30:50,269
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, there was that sutta, it said that anāgāmī is doing it.

434
00:30:50,289 --> 00:30:50,729
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

435
00:30:50,789 --> 00:30:59,795
[SPEAKER_05]: So, he has no wish for heavens or rewards or sensual benefits or something, but he's doing it for a completely different reason that's invisible to those without the right view.

436
00:31:00,335 --> 00:31:02,897
[SPEAKER_05]: And that reason is, oh, it ornates my mind.

437
00:31:03,077 --> 00:31:05,479
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the requisite and adornment of the mind, isn't it?

438
00:31:05,499 --> 00:31:05,599
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

439
00:31:05,619 --> 00:31:14,127
[SPEAKER_05]: It's basically by engaging in generosity towards others, effortlessly, this mind is embellished beautifully.

440
00:31:14,567 --> 00:31:16,929
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, I don't have to bear it.

441
00:31:17,750 --> 00:31:18,971
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not difficult for me.

442
00:31:19,071 --> 00:31:20,252
[SPEAKER_05]: It's not unpleasant to bear it.

443
00:31:20,332 --> 00:31:27,798
[SPEAKER_05]: It's much more easier to bear the mind that's ornate and light and embellished and wholesome.

444
00:31:28,419 --> 00:31:29,420
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you realize, instead of

445
00:31:30,729 --> 00:31:42,914
[SPEAKER_05]: It's basically like the... almost recognizing the... which is more efficient effort to elevate your mind, so that you see it clearly and... and exist with it until it breaks apart more peacefully.

446
00:31:44,014 --> 00:31:55,258
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you can... you can make this effort, you can make that effort, but generosity... oh, that takes very little, yet the mind is directly, as a result of it, embellished and elevated.

447
00:31:56,079 --> 00:31:59,060
[SPEAKER_05]: And he sees that directly and he does it for that reason and no other.

448
00:32:00,472 --> 00:32:03,694
[SPEAKER_05]: No, no, no desire for reward, no desire for thank you for that matter.

449
00:32:03,714 --> 00:32:04,495
[SPEAKER_05]: It doesn't matter.

450
00:32:04,655 --> 00:32:04,975
[SPEAKER_05]: Absolutely.

451
00:32:04,995 --> 00:32:05,775
[SPEAKER_05]: It makes no difference.

452
00:32:06,776 --> 00:32:09,658
[SPEAKER_05]: All he wants is his own mind to be elevated on the result of it.

453
00:32:10,478 --> 00:32:22,786
[SPEAKER_00]: And in process without his benefit as well, so... That's the thing, like, when you're... if you're doing it for somebody else, now, if they... if they are ungrateful or if they don't like it or something, I'm affected by that.

454
00:32:22,806 --> 00:32:24,287
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so you realize, oh, just stop doing it.

455
00:32:24,307 --> 00:32:24,667
[SPEAKER_05]: No, no, no.

456
00:32:24,907 --> 00:32:27,469
[SPEAKER_05]: You carry on doing it, but now learn how not to be affected by it.

457
00:32:28,317 --> 00:32:38,062
[SPEAKER_05]: Because there was exactly preceding was, you know, they do it for the reward, they do it for the heavens, and then to the point of, oh, he just does it for the sake of his own mind.

458
00:32:39,123 --> 00:32:45,066
[SPEAKER_05]: But it is factually good, generosity also, but it's still worldly.

459
00:32:45,146 --> 00:32:49,148
[SPEAKER_05]: Merit still partakes in the world, in rebirth, in saṃsāra.

460
00:32:50,489 --> 00:32:54,311
[SPEAKER_05]: So it's not the question of, oh, don't do it because you can't do it purely.

461
00:32:54,531 --> 00:32:56,752
[SPEAKER_05]: You won't be able to do it purely because you're not anāgāmī.

462
00:32:58,017 --> 00:32:58,798
[SPEAKER_05]: You're not an arahant.

463
00:33:00,399 --> 00:33:06,523
[SPEAKER_05]: So, do it whichever way you can, and then what comes as a result of it, you learn how not to be perturbed in regard to it.

464
00:33:06,663 --> 00:33:13,227
[SPEAKER_05]: Whether it's the expectations of heaven, or being upset because you didn't have enough thank you or gratitude, that's all secondary.

465
00:33:13,908 --> 00:33:19,532
[SPEAKER_05]: You keep doing it, and then you deal with these things on the level of the mind in a sense of learn how not to be perturbed by those things.

466
00:33:21,693 --> 00:33:22,774
[SPEAKER_05]: And then you will actually grow.

467
00:33:23,552 --> 00:33:26,534
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, and how, when safeguarding others, do you safeguard yourself?

468
00:33:26,914 --> 00:33:36,301
[SPEAKER_01]: With yoniso, manasikāra, a mind of metta, harmlessness, and patient endurance.

469
00:33:37,282 --> 00:33:39,183
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's all those internal practices.

470
00:33:39,203 --> 00:33:40,664
[SPEAKER_05]: It's what we've been describing, basically.

471
00:33:41,885 --> 00:33:43,346
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's just one more question.

472
00:33:43,866 --> 00:33:52,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Even when I know that my intentions are bad, or that my actions will lead to more suffering, I still don't stop cultivating them.

473
00:33:52,948 --> 00:33:55,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, it seems that I don't really care about myself.

474
00:33:56,873 --> 00:34:04,001
[SPEAKER_01]: If I practice metta towards myself, will I be able to not choose bad actions next time the intention is there?

475
00:34:04,121 --> 00:34:05,683
[SPEAKER_05]: Putting the cart in front of the horse.

476
00:34:07,685 --> 00:34:11,630
[SPEAKER_05]: Practice of metta will not make you be able to stop choosing badly.

477
00:34:12,171 --> 00:34:13,092
[SPEAKER_05]: Not choosing badly,

478
00:34:13,512 --> 00:34:15,835
[SPEAKER_05]: will enable you to stop choosing badly.

479
00:34:15,895 --> 00:34:17,056
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you can practice metta.

480
00:34:17,396 --> 00:34:18,938
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in a way, you're cultivating metta.

481
00:34:18,978 --> 00:34:23,382
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, that's closer to practice of metta than doing metta, so that this is dealt from the same.

482
00:34:23,803 --> 00:34:35,635
[SPEAKER_05]: People want, oh, if I get the jhāna, the pleasure of jhāna that's contemporarily taught, then I will just stop being attracted to sensual pleasures, but I won't have to go through the pains of withdrawal and overcoming them.

483
00:34:36,296 --> 00:34:39,278
[SPEAKER_05]: I just need to get this, so that then this magically falls away.

484
00:34:39,898 --> 00:34:45,382
[SPEAKER_05]: So, if I do metta, my bad choices, that I choose, will magically just stop happening.

485
00:34:46,422 --> 00:34:46,582
[SPEAKER_03]: No?

486
00:34:47,003 --> 00:34:49,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, my decisions will change.

487
00:34:49,624 --> 00:34:49,804
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

488
00:34:49,844 --> 00:35:00,111
[SPEAKER_05]: So, you want to do metta rightly, you start by not cultivating the choices you already, quite clearly, quite well, understand you shouldn't be doing.

489
00:35:00,751 --> 00:35:02,672
[SPEAKER_05]: And you don't care about yourself at the time.

490
00:35:02,792 --> 00:35:03,373
[SPEAKER_05]: And that's correct.

491
00:35:03,893 --> 00:35:05,274
[SPEAKER_05]: You obviously lose the context.

492
00:35:06,246 --> 00:35:07,107
[SPEAKER_05]: So, what do I do then?

493
00:35:07,147 --> 00:35:14,571
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, contemplate the danger, peril, obviously, sense restraint, if you haven't developed it.

494
00:35:15,692 --> 00:35:18,954
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, yeah, the peril will be too much to ignore.

495
00:35:21,856 --> 00:35:23,037
[SPEAKER_05]: And then, you won't make such choices.

496
00:35:23,057 --> 00:35:23,857
[SPEAKER_05]: You go with it.

497
00:35:26,239 --> 00:35:27,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And this...

498
00:35:28,000 --> 00:35:32,463
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought of bringing up this sutta as well, because it's a how-do-you-know-when's-good-for-you kind of thing.

499
00:35:36,816 --> 00:35:39,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people, they keep on doing bad things.

500
00:35:41,238 --> 00:35:45,140
[SPEAKER_01]: But if they knew that those were bad things, they wouldn't do it.

501
00:35:46,041 --> 00:35:48,002
[SPEAKER_01]: But in some way, they think those things are good.

502
00:35:48,382 --> 00:35:51,723
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, it brings me pleasure, here and now, later on.

503
00:35:51,763 --> 00:35:54,405
[SPEAKER_05]: Or they don't see the extent of how bad they are.

504
00:35:54,605 --> 00:35:56,886
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, yeah, it's bad, but...

505
00:35:58,407 --> 00:36:01,388
[SPEAKER_05]: But if they were to see the extent of the peril, the extent of the danger,

506
00:36:02,453 --> 00:36:05,135
[SPEAKER_05]: Then they would realize, oh, I cannot engage with this.

507
00:36:06,656 --> 00:36:10,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, and just to bring up this sutta, which is, how do you see that?

508
00:36:12,721 --> 00:36:19,927
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's the Rāhula, one of the Rāhula suttas, and the Buddha asks Rāhula, what is a mirror for?

509
00:36:22,209 --> 00:36:24,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And Rāhula answers for reflection.

510
00:36:25,787 --> 00:36:35,771
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha says, in the same way, Rahula, bodily actions, verbal actions and mental actions are to be done with repeated reflection or consideration.

511
00:36:36,731 --> 00:36:43,413
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever you want to do a bodily, verbal or mental act, you should reflect on it.

512
00:36:44,033 --> 00:36:49,315
[SPEAKER_01]: This act I want to do, would it lead to self-affliction, to the affliction of others or to both?

513
00:36:49,996 --> 00:36:51,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Would it be unskillful?

514
00:36:51,556 --> 00:36:53,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Would it be an unskillful action resulting in dukkha?

515
00:36:54,972 --> 00:37:06,385
[SPEAKER_01]: If on reflection you know that it would lead to self-affliction, to the affliction of others, or to both, it would be an unskillful bodily action resulting in dukkha.

516
00:37:06,946 --> 00:37:11,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Then any action of that sort is absolutely unfit for you to do.

517
00:37:15,191 --> 00:37:28,854
[SPEAKER_01]: And the opposite is, if you reflect that these actions will not lead to your self-affliction, to another's affliction, or to both, it would be skillful action resulting in happiness.

518
00:37:28,974 --> 00:37:31,895
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, any action of that sort is fit for you to do.

519
00:37:34,736 --> 00:37:40,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And, at the end, he says, Rahula, all those contemplatives in the past, future, present,

520
00:37:41,175 --> 00:37:48,944
[SPEAKER_01]: And all who purified their bodily, verbal and mental actions, did it through this repeated reflection on their actions, in just this way.

521
00:37:48,964 --> 00:37:49,544
[SPEAKER_05]: No other way.

522
00:37:50,525 --> 00:37:52,327
[SPEAKER_05]: So, there is no magical thing you can do.

523
00:37:52,748 --> 00:37:54,950
[SPEAKER_05]: They will then purify those things for you in return.

524
00:37:55,951 --> 00:37:57,333
[SPEAKER_03]: Simply doesn't exist.

525
00:37:57,373 --> 00:37:58,594
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, it exists is a wrong view.

526
00:37:58,775 --> 00:37:58,975
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

527
00:37:59,968 --> 00:38:05,933
[SPEAKER_05]: But, practically, whoever has ever done it successfully, they did it through that way.

528
00:38:06,333 --> 00:38:07,214
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha himself did.

529
00:38:07,234 --> 00:38:08,194
[SPEAKER_05]: As the Buddha himself did.

530
00:38:08,414 --> 00:38:10,216
[SPEAKER_05]: Purifying his mind from the unwholesome states.

531
00:38:10,956 --> 00:38:11,217
[SPEAKER_05]: How?

532
00:38:11,237 --> 00:38:24,527
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, when there is a sensual lust present, he would establish the context of danger, peril, lack of satisfaction, not beneficial for him, for others, and then the thought would subside, whenever it subsides.

533
00:38:24,687 --> 00:38:25,688
[SPEAKER_05]: That was not his concern.

534
00:38:25,948 --> 00:38:28,630
[SPEAKER_05]: His concern was to protect the context, the framework.

535
00:38:29,775 --> 00:38:46,085
[SPEAKER_05]: Ill-will, thoughts of hatred, context again, of how little benefit that has, how it increases burden for you, how unbeneficial it is, how friendliness is a much better option, letting go is a better option, how all these contexts he would cultivate in face of these thoughts.

536
00:38:47,767 --> 00:38:49,047
[SPEAKER_05]: And then they would subside eventually.

537
00:38:50,768 --> 00:38:53,110
[SPEAKER_05]: So yoniso manasikāra keeps the context.

538
00:38:55,091 --> 00:38:57,853
[SPEAKER_05]: Endurance, that we often talk about, khanti,

539
00:38:58,420 --> 00:39:01,202
[SPEAKER_05]: Forbearance, patient endurance, whichever you want to translate it.

540
00:39:01,843 --> 00:39:08,429
[SPEAKER_05]: Oh, so you're enduring the presence of this thought, which is pretty much the benchmark for the context to be developed.

541
00:39:09,930 --> 00:39:12,872
[SPEAKER_05]: Until the thought subsides, not until you got rid of it.

542
00:39:14,394 --> 00:39:15,635
[SPEAKER_05]: I got rid of what bothered me.

543
00:39:16,035 --> 00:39:17,577
[SPEAKER_05]: No, you were not bothered by it.

544
00:39:17,957 --> 00:39:19,118
[SPEAKER_05]: Why are you trying to get rid of it?

545
00:39:19,578 --> 00:39:21,480
[SPEAKER_05]: You're bothered by your own craving in regard to it.

546
00:39:22,838 --> 00:39:23,438
[SPEAKER_05]: Get rid of that.

547
00:39:23,998 --> 00:39:25,679
[SPEAKER_05]: But good luck getting rid of that directly.

548
00:39:25,859 --> 00:39:27,139
[SPEAKER_05]: Attending it or replacing it.

549
00:39:27,259 --> 00:39:27,699
[SPEAKER_05]: Impossible.

550
00:39:27,719 --> 00:39:28,399
[SPEAKER_05]: Could you be doing that?

551
00:39:28,419 --> 00:39:28,939
[SPEAKER_05]: Be craving.

552
00:39:30,820 --> 00:39:36,721
[SPEAKER_03]: But then you could do that with... Like I said, the thought would subside on its own.

553
00:39:37,161 --> 00:39:44,043
[SPEAKER_03]: But it's very easy to fall into... Like I established the context so that I get rid of this thought because it's bothering me.

554
00:39:44,123 --> 00:39:45,383
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, so there won't be a context.

555
00:39:45,564 --> 00:39:46,064
[SPEAKER_05]: Yeah, exactly.

556
00:39:46,104 --> 00:39:49,765
[SPEAKER_03]: But so then what would be a better intention?

557
00:39:49,805 --> 00:39:51,505
[SPEAKER_05]: Well, to recognize that you've been doing that,

558
00:39:51,885 --> 00:39:53,386
[SPEAKER_05]: And now, upgrade that as well.

559
00:39:53,486 --> 00:39:55,007
[SPEAKER_05]: Establish the context in regard to that.

560
00:39:55,887 --> 00:39:57,148
[SPEAKER_05]: You won't start perfectly.

561
00:39:57,728 --> 00:39:59,529
[SPEAKER_05]: You will be wanting to get rid of the pain.

562
00:39:59,689 --> 00:39:59,889
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

